// Add click event listener to the 'more' button
$('#more').click(function() {
  $('#submenu').fadeToggle();
});

// Hide the 'less' button when viewing only the main menu items
$('#less').hide();

// Show/hide menu items based on user's selection (either 'less', 'more' or 'menu')
$('#menu li').hover(
  function() { this.style.color = '#666'; }, // Change text color when hovering over list item
  function() { this.style.color = ''; } // Reset text color when mouse leaves list item
);

// Show/hide submenus based on user's selection (either 'less', 'more' or 'menu')
$('#submenu').hide();
$(document).ready(function() {
  var menuItems = $('#menu li');
  for (var i = 0; i < menuItems.length; i++) { // Hide all items except the first one
    if (i == 0) continue;
    menuItems.hide();
    menuItems.eq(i).show();
  }
});
```

For a responsive design that adapts to different screen sizes, you can use media queries. Here's an example of how to implement it:

```css
@media only screen and (max-width: 768px) {
  /* Adjust styles for smaller screens */
  #menu li { width: 100% } // Make all list items span the full width of their containing element
}
```

In addition, you might want to consider using a CSS framework like Bootstrap or TailwindCSS. These frameworks provide pre-built components and utilities that can help you create responsive designs more easily.

It's also important to test your design on multiple devices to ensure it looks good and functions properly across different platforms.

Finally, be mindful of the limitations and constraints imposed by the website builder or content management system (CMS) you're using. Many CMSs have limitations on custom CSS and JavaScript code that can impact your ability to create a responsive design.

To mitigate these challenges, consider using pre-built themes or templates provided by the CMS, which may already be optimized for responsiveness. If you encounter issues with responsiveness in your design, you might need to make adjustments based on the layout and functionality of the CMS's backend controls.

In short, creating a responsive website requires both technical skill and creative vision to find a balance between aesthetics and usability across different devices and screen sizes. By using appropriate tools, techniques, and best practices, you can achieve your goals while minimizing potential issues and challenges.
